    92	The Hamiltonian considered in the seed is
    93	
    94	\[
    95	H_N=\Phi_N\Phi_N^\dagger.
    96	\]
    97	
    98	With the Fock inner product, the elementary kernel identity is
    99	
   100	\[
   101	\ker H_N=(I_N)^\perp,
   102	\qquad
   103	\dim\ker H_N=\operatorname{HF}_{R/I}(N).
   104	\]
   105	
   106	The same space is the degree-\(N\) Macaulay inverse system.
   107	
   108	Define
   109	
   110	\[
   111	\Delta_N=\lambda_{\min}^{\neq0}(H_N)
   112	        =\sigma_{\min}^{\neq0}(\Phi_N)^2.
   113	\]
   114	
   115	A sparse-access block encoding has some normalization \(\alpha_N\geq\|H_N\|\).
   116	
   117	The relevant spectral parameter is
   118	
   119	\[
   120	\gamma_N=\frac{\Delta_N}{\alpha_N},
   121	\]
   122	
   123	not the unnormalized gap \(\Delta_N\).
   124	
   125	A QSVT approximation to the kernel projector has query complexity
   126	
   127	\[
   128	\widetilde O\!\left(\gamma_N^{-1}\log(1/\epsilon)\right).
   129	\]
   130	
   131	This excludes row-oracle construction, reversible arithmetic, preparation of the input state, overlap amplification, and readout.
   132	
   133	The occupation-number register needs \(O(n\log(N+1))\) qubits.
   134	
   135	That compact register does not imply running time polynomial in \(\log N\): entries and normalization generally grow as \(N^{O(d)}\).
   136	
   137	A classical sparse matrix-vector multiplication by \(\Phi_N\), \(\Phi_N^\dagger\), or \(H_N\) costs \(O(\operatorname{nnz})\) arithmetic operations if the matrix is materialized.
