   203	### Problem statements
   204	
   205	Exact ideal membership asks whether a given \(g\in K[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\), of degree \(e\), lies in \(I\).
   206	
   207	The output is one bit, optionally accompanied by a certificate
   208	
   209	\[
   210	g=\sum_i h_i f_i.
   211	\]
   212	
   213	Homogeneous degree-local membership assumes \(g\in R_N\) and asks whether \(g\in I_N\).
   214	
   215	Approximate degree-local membership fixes an inner product and promises either
   216	
   217	\[
   218	g\in I_N
   219	\]
   220	
   221	or
   222	
   223	\[
   224	\operatorname{dist}(g,I_N)\geq\epsilon\|g\|.
   225	\]
   226	
   227	Estimating
   228	
   229	\[
   230	\delta_I(g)^2
   231	 =\frac{\|(I-P_{I_N})g\|^2}{\|g\|^2}
   232	 =\frac{\langle g|P_{\ker H_N}|g\rangle}{\|g\|^2}
   233	\]
   234	
   235	is a different numerical problem.
   236	
   237	### Best classical algorithms
   238	
   239	Given a Gröbner basis \(G\), exact membership is decided by reducing \(g\) to its unique normal form.
   240	
   241	The expensive step is normally computing \(G\).
   242	
   243	Buchberger, F4, F5, and signature-based algorithms are the standard general methods.
   244	
   245	Macaulay2 and Singular provide robust general ideal and module operations.
   360	Input size must include \(n,s,d,t,\tau\), and output size must include the degrees and coefficient heights of the \(h_i\).
   361	
   362	### Best classical algorithms
   363	
   364	Rabinowitsch's trick gives
   365	
   366	\[
   367	g\in\sqrt I
   368	\iff
   369	1\in I+(1-y g)
   370	\subset K[x_1,\ldots,x_n,y].
   371	\]
   372	
   373	Thus radical membership reduces to ordinary ideal membership in one more variable.
   374	
   375	General algorithms use Gröbner bases, saturation, elimination, primary decomposition, or triangular decompositions.
   376	
   377	Singular and Macaulay2 implement radical, saturation, associated-prime, and primary-decomposition workflows.
   378	
   379	For zero-dimensional ideals, one can construct quotient multiplication matrices and remove nilpotent structure by square-free factorization or compute the radical during FGLM conversion.
   380	
   381	`msolve` computes a lexicographic representation of the radical in its generic zero-dimensional solving pipeline [R11].
   382	
   383	### Complexity and certificates
   384	
   385	Complex feasibility with integer coefficients is NP-hard.
   386	
   387	It is unconditionally in PSPACE.
   388	
   389	Under the generalized Riemann hypothesis it lies in \(\mathrm{AM}\), more precisely in \(\mathrm{RP}^{\mathrm{NP}}\) in Koiran's formulation [R07].
   390	
   391	Therefore emptiness is coNP-hard and is in coAM under the same hypothesis.
   392	
   393	Setting \(g=1\) shows that radical membership is coNP-hard.
   394	
   395	Kollár's effective Nullstellensatz gives certificate degrees bounded singly exponentially in \(n\); a safe uniform scale for degree-\(d\) inputs is \(d^{O(n)}\), with sharp product-of-degrees versions [R06].
   396	
   397	The resulting Macaulay matrix can therefore have
   398	
   399	\[
   400	\binom{n+d^{\Theta(n)}}{n}
   401	\]
   402	
   403	columns.
   404	
   405	Its succinct occupation encoding does not make the corresponding QSVT runtime polynomial, because normalization and arithmetic depend polynomially on the exponentially large degree.
   406	
   407	### Seed structure and quantum assessment
   408	
   409	The Hamiltonian kernel represents \(I_N^\perp\), not \(\sqrt I_N^\perp\).
   410	
   411	Nilpotent scheme structure produces additional, generally entangled, inverse-system vectors.
   412	
   413	Coherent product states correspond only to geometric points.
   414	
   415	The coherent-state expectation
   416	
   417	\[
   418	\langle p^{\otimes N}|H_N|p^{\otimes N}\rangle
   419	=
   420	\sum_i\frac{N!}{(N-d_i)!}|f_i(\bar p)|^2
   421	\]
   422	
   423	does not give a quantum speedup for testing a supplied point.
   424	
   425	The right side is classically evaluable in \(O(t)\) arithmetic operations.
   426	
   427	A radical-membership algorithm would have to prove that \(g\) vanishes on all coherent ground states, while distinguishing them from nilpotent inverse-system states.
   428	
   429	No efficient operation supplied by the seed performs that separation.
   430	
   431	General Nullstellensatz feasibility also reaches truncation degrees \(d^{\Theta(n)}\), making the seed construction exponentially costly in time.
   432	
   433	A quantum advantage remains conceivable for a promise version with:
   434	
   435	- polynomial certificate degree;
   436	- inverse-polynomial normalized gap;
   437	- an efficiently prepared witness distribution;
   438	- additive rather than exact output.
   439	
   440	No such general promise class is currently established.
   441	
   442	### Cheapest hardware attack
   443	
   444	A variational coherent-state search minimizes \(\sum_i|f_i(p)|^2\) directly.
   445	
   446	For quadratics this maps naturally to spinor condensates or coupled Bose-Hubbard modes.
   447	
   448	It is a heuristic nonconvex optimizer, not a Nullstellensatz certificate algorithm.
   449	
   450	MBQC is the most flexible route for an actual decision circuit.
   451	
   452	Linear optics with postselection can test small certificate matrices, but an exponentially small postselection probability would merely re-express the classical hardness.
   453	
