   454	## 5. Hilbert function, Hilbert polynomial, dimension, and degree
   455	
   456	### Problem statements
   457	
   458	For a homogeneous ideal \(I\), compute
   459	
   460	\[
   461	\operatorname{HF}_{R/I}(N)=\dim_K(R/I)_N.
   462	\]
   463	
   464	The exact Hilbert-series problem asks for
   465	
   466	\[
   467	\operatorname{HS}_{R/I}(z)
   468	 =\sum_{N\geq0}\operatorname{HF}_{R/I}(N)z^N.
   469	\]
   470	
   471	The eventual polynomial \(P_I(N)\) determines projective dimension and degree:
   472	
   473	\[
   474	\deg P_I=\dim\operatorname{Proj}(R/I),
   475	\]
   476	
   477	and the leading coefficient is \(\deg(V)/(\dim V)!\).
   478	
   479	The normalized approximate problem asks for
   480	
   481	\[
   482	h_N=\frac{\operatorname{HF}_{R/I}(N)}{M_N}
   483	\]
   484	
   485	to additive error \(\epsilon\).
   486	
   487	These outputs have radically different complexity.
   488	
   489	### Best classical algorithms
   490	
   491	At a fixed \(N\),
   492	
   493	\[
   494	\operatorname{HF}_{R/I}(N)=M_N-\operatorname{rank}\Phi_N.
   495	\]
   496	
   497	This can be computed by exact dense or sparse rank.
   498	
   499	For many values of \(N\), standard practice computes a Gröbner basis, replaces \(I\) by its initial monomial ideal, and counts standard monomials.
   500	
   501	Macaulay2 and Singular compute Hilbert functions, series, polynomials, dimension, multiplicity, and degree from this data.
   502	
   503	For monomial ideals, combinatorial algorithms, Stanley decompositions, lcm structures, and rational generating functions can avoid general Gröbner work.
   504	
   505	For a complete intersection of degrees \(d_1,\ldots,d_s\), the Hilbert series is known explicitly:
   506	
   507	\[
   508	\operatorname{HS}_{R/I}(z)
   509	=
   510	\frac{\prod_i(1-z^{d_i})}{(1-z)^n}.
   511	\]
   512	
   513	A quantum algorithm has no baseline advantage on such inputs.
   514	
   515	For generic overdetermined forms, the analogous truncation predicted by Fröberg remains conjectural in full generality [R14].
   516	
   517	### Complexity and hardness
   518	
   519	Exact evaluation of Hilbert series is #P-hard even for initial ideals associated with radical zero-dimensional complete intersections [R15].
   520	
   521	Computing the general Hilbert polynomial is polynomial-space hard [R16].
   522	
   523	Boolean equations \(x_i^2-x_i=0\) and clause polynomials give a direct parsimonious route from #SAT to eventual quotient dimension.
   524	
   525	Thus exact stable Hilbert function, exact degree of a zero-dimensional radical ideal, and exact root count can encode #P-hard counting.
   526	
   527	The output itself can require \(\Theta(n)\) bits even when the root count is \(2^n\), so this is not merely an output-length obstruction.
   528	
   529	### Seed structure and quantum assessment
   530	
   531	The kernel degeneracy is exactly the Hilbert function.
   532	
   533	A DQC1-style trace estimate of a QSVT kernel projector targets
   534	
   535	\[
   536	\frac{\operatorname{Tr}P_{\ker H_N}}{M_N}=h_N.
   537	\]
   538	
   539	The schematic quantum sample cost is \(O(\epsilon^{-2})\), or \(O(\epsilon^{-1})\) with coherent amplitude estimation, times the projector cost.
   540	
   541	This must be compared with classical stochastic trace estimation.
   542	
   543	Given an efficient routine for \(v\mapsto P_{\ker H_N}v\), Hutchinson, Hutch++, or stochastic Lanczos also avoids explicit diagonalization [R54, R55].
   544	
   545	Hutch++ obtains relative trace error with \(O(1/\epsilon)\) matrix-vector products for PSD inputs [R55].
   546	
   547	The possible advantage is therefore in the cost of applying the filter, not simply in “quantum trace estimation.”
   548	
   549	For a codimension-\(c\) projective variety of degree \(D\) in the stable range,
   550	
   551	\[
   552	h_N\asymp
   553	D\,\frac{(n-1)!}{(n-1-c)!}\,N^{-c}.
   554	\]
   555	
   556	Recovering \(D\) requires error below roughly half the change caused by incrementing \(D\), hence \(\epsilon=O(N^{-c})\).
   557	
   558	This eliminates polylogarithmic dependence on \(N\).
   559	
   560	A plausible speedup remains for structured low-codimension ideals where \(N=\operatorname{poly}(n)\), \(c=O(1)\), and classical methods still require vectors of length \(M_N\).
   561	
   562	Exact Hilbert functions and multiplicative estimates that distinguish zero from nonzero remain #P/NP-hard targets and are not credible general BQP tasks.
   563	
   564	### Cheapest hardware attack
   565	
   566	Ground-space spectroscopy is the direct condensed-matter experiment.
   567	
   568	The difficulty is that degeneracy counting requires temperature and energy resolution below \(\Delta_N\), and readout of an exponentially large degeneracy is not automatic.
   569	
   570	For small quadratic ideals, Bose-Hubbard or spinor-BEC platforms are natural.
   571	
   572	A photonic experiment could estimate normalized traces through randomized measurements or interferometric overlap tests, but generic \(H_N\) still needs non-Gaussian interactions.
   573	
   574	Boson sampling does not natively return kernel dimension.
