   803	## 8. Distance to a variety and nearest points
   804	
   805	### Problem statements
   806	
   807	Given a real algebraic variety
   808	
   809	\[
   810	X=\{x\in\mathbb R^n:f_1(x)=\cdots=f_s(x)=0\}
   811	\]
   812	
   813	and \(u\in\mathbb Q^n\), compute
   814	
   815	\[
   816	\operatorname{dist}(u,X)^2
   817	 =\min_{x\in X}\|x-u\|_2^2.
   818	\]
   819	
   820	Possible outputs are:
   821	
   822	- the global minimum value to additive error;
   823	- a nearest real point;
   824	- all complex critical points;
   825	- the Euclidean-distance degree of \(X\).
   826	
   827	The Euclidean-distance degree is the number of complex critical points for generic \(u\) [R25].
   828	
   829	### Best classical algorithms
   830	
   831	For smooth complete intersections, Lagrange multipliers give
   832	
   833	\[
   834	f_i(x)=0,\qquad
   835	x-u=J_f(x)^\top\lambda.
   836	\]
   837	
   838	Symbolic Gröbner or resultant methods solve this critical system exactly.
   839	
   840	Numerical homotopy tracks its ED-degree many solutions.
   841	
   842	Monodromy and local critical-point methods can reduce path work in families [R26].
   843	
   844	Macaulay2 has an `EuclideanDistanceDegree` package.
   845	
   846	Bertini and HomotopyContinuation.jl solve the critical systems numerically.
   847	
   848	For a generic affine hypersurface of degree \(d\) in \(\mathbb C^n\),
   849	
   850	\[
   851	\operatorname{EDdeg}(X)
   852	 =d\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}(d-1)^i,
   853	\]
   854	
   855	so the all-critical-point problem is already exponential in \(n\) at fixed \(d>2\) [R25].
   856	
   857	Special varieties can be much easier.
   858	
   859	For unconstrained low-rank matrices, the nearest rank-\(r\) point is given by truncated SVD.
   860	
   861	No quantum algorithm should be compared with generic polynomial solving on that case.
   862	
   863	### Hardness
   864	
   865	General polynomial distance minimization contains polynomial optimization and is NP-hard.
   866	
   867	Nearest rank-one approximation and spectral norm for order-three tensors are NP-hard, including approximation variants [R27].
   868	
   869	There may be no well-separated unique nearest point.
   870	
   871	Near the ED discriminant, critical points collide and the condition number diverges.
   872	
   873	### Seed structure and quantum assessment
   874	
   875	For a coherent state, \(H_N\)'s energy is a weighted residual sum of squares.
   876	
   877	Thus coherent-state energy minimization approximates distance to the zero set after normalization and metric corrections.
   878	
   879	The full ground space is larger than the coherent-state manifold.
   880	
   881	Ground-state preparation therefore does not solve nearest-point optimization.
   882	
   883	A plausible construction forms the Lagrange critical ideal and applies the zero-dimensional quotient machinery.
   884	
   885	If the ED degree \(D_{\rm ED}\) is exponential, a quantum spectral sampler might return one critical point without enumerating all \(D_{\rm ED}\) paths.
   886	
   887	The gain survives only if:
   888	
   889	- the critical ideal is polynomially gapped;
   890	- a root-supported state has nonnegligible overlap;
   891	- roots are well separated;
   892	- the desired nearest real critical point can be identified without reading all roots.
   893	
   894	Finding the minimum among \(D_{\rm ED}\) opaque critical values is at best a quadratic Grover-type improvement without further geometry.
   895	
   896	### Cheapest hardware attack
   897	
   898	The most direct heuristic is variational coherent-state minimization in a Bose-Hubbard or spinor-condensate emulator.
   899	
   900	Quadratic varieties are the practical first test.
   901	
   902	Photonic MBQC can implement the Lagrange-system projector for small examples.
   903	
   904	Boson sampling is relevant for distance to special determinantal or matching varieties only when the critical equations reduce to permanent or hafnian statistics.
   905	

